buildroot/nodejs: pure cross-compile for riscv64 (no qemu-user), bump to 22.22.0

Buildroot's stock nodejs package cross-compiles by building a *target* mksnapshot
and running it under qemu-user, and its arch allowlist has no riscv at all. Our
build policy forbids executing target binaries at build time, so riscv64 gets the
same-width host-toolset path instead: V8's host tools are compiled with the HOST
toolchain and run natively. V8 enables its RISC-V simulator by itself in that
configuration, so an x86-64 mksnapshot serializes a riscv64 snapshot.

Measured before writing this patch (Node v22.22.0, x86-64 host, SDK Xuantie glibc
GCC 10.2 target): mksnapshot/torque/bytecode_builtins_list_generator all build as
x86-64 ELF, the snapshot action emits a 6.5MB riscv64 embedded.S in 1.08s, and the
build trace contains zero qemu invocations and zero target execution. Mechanism,
measurements and traps: docs/BUILDROOT.md.

The riscv64 behaviour changes are gated on BR2_RISCV_64, so arm/aarch64/mips/x86
keep the stock qemu path. The version and hash changes are global by nature.

Two traps this patch exists to avoid, both measured rather than assumed:

  * The ninja generator cannot be combined with want_separate_host_toolset --
    v8_inspector_headers is declared for both toolsets but writes into the shared
    gen/ directory, so both emit the same js_protocol.stamp edge and ninja aborts.
    riscv64 therefore builds with the GYP make generator.

  * gyp does NOT bake the configure-time CC_host into the generated out/Makefile;
    it emits a literal "CC.host ?= cc" plus a TODO admitting the env fallback is
    replicated in make. Since make cannot read an env var named "CC.host", the host
    toolset must be passed to the build step as make command-line variables. A build
    that only sets CC_host at configure time silently uses whatever /usr/bin/cc is.

Also bumps the package to Node 22.22.0 -- the version our product contract pins and
the one SMHub ships -- refreshing every entry in nodejs.hash from the upstream
SHASUMS256.txt, including the prebuilt-binary rows that host-nodejs-bin consumes.

